00:33:20,880 --> 00:33:29,760
Actually, I wonder in some cases it might erode that perceived trust, you know, and over time, like it's no longer like breaking between Walter Kongkite, you know, to sell you Colgate.

393
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:31,440
Like it feels like it's a little bit more accessible.

394
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,040
So I wonder if that's going to change.

395
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:35,680
The same is for terrestrial radio too.

396
00:33:35,920 --> 00:33:42,240
I so often I hear Babel and Noom and like so many terrestrial radio buys.

397
00:33:42,320 --> 00:33:48,640
And I mean, you probably know this, but like I've heard it's depending on how you target can be cheaper than you you think, especially probably at your scale.

398
00:33:48,800 --> 00:33:53,280
You know, if you're spending five figures on Facebook a day, it's like probably not that crazy.

399
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:57,840
Yeah, I think that TV and radio are different today than they were.

400
00:33:58,400 --> 00:34:04,400
Not to make this a political thing, but during the Trump administration, TV was at its height.

401
00:34:04,560 --> 00:34:06,400
You know, it was doing really well.

402
00:34:06,880 --> 00:34:13,520
Yeah, whether it's Fox News or CNN or in the CNBC, everyone's just glued to the you mean for effectiveness even.

403
00:34:13,760 --> 00:34:15,840
I guess costs probably went up along with that too, right?

404
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:17,760
I think the ratios were good.

405
00:34:18,080 --> 00:34:19,920
If you had a good brand, which we do.

406
00:34:20,160 --> 00:34:21,840
So it worked, it resonated well.

407
00:34:22,240 --> 00:34:25,280
We worked with some of the biggest TV buying agencies.

408
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:29,760
And they were blown away by the effectiveness of our app advertisements that we can actually get.

409
00:34:30,080 --> 00:34:30,720
Well, it's unique.

410
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:31,840
It's like you don't see an app.

411
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:38,080
It's like the juxtaposition of two things like technology and faith, which I mean, exists, but it's like somewhat novel, right?

412
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:39,200
The combination of those things.

413
00:34:39,360 --> 00:34:44,720
So it doesn't surprise me, at least be intriguing compared to your twentieth fitness app or like whatever, right?

414
00:34:44,880 --> 00:34:46,000
It's like something somewhat new.

415
00:34:46,240 --> 00:34:47,280
So it's pretty interesting.

416
00:34:47,600 --> 00:34:47,680
Yeah.

417
00:34:47,920 --> 00:34:50,560
And the creatives were a little bit different, but they weren't too much.

418
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:52,960
I mean, we had James Earl Jones reads the Bible.

419
00:34:53,280 --> 00:35:01,200
And so that helps with creatives, but we ran other ones that didn't, didn't have James Earl Jones that our content team put together some bangers.

420
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:09,120
And so they were able to perform really well and set us up for understanding the media mix models, but TV buying, radio buying, totally different animal.

421
00:35:09,360 --> 00:35:13,520
You're making big bets on prime times and in these kind of earlier shows.

422
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:32,720
And then you have to have a 50% strategy covering 50% of your spend that's very well spread out across many channels, time slots, so that you can have some consistencies because a Tuesday prime time one week may just be the best thing you've ever seen and maybe the worst thing you've ever seen the next week.

423
00:35:32,880 --> 00:35:40,240
So it's very volatile and you just have to understand that volatility and bring some calm to that turbulence.

424
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:43,120
Yeah, I was going to ask, how does your team divide?

425
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:48,000
your focus between like customer engagement and product development, right?

426
00:35:48,240 --> 00:36:01,440
Like actually on what you build versus like, you know, your internal optimization around paywalls and stuff like that versus like, it feels like this whole performance marketing and advertising and acquisition, that's probably like a whole technology stack, right?

427
00:36:01,760 --> 00:36:08,640
Like how do you like as leadership team or whatever, however, you think about, you know, your what your pie of corporate focus, like how do you break it down?

428
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:12,960
Like how much resourcing and thought goes into the different sort of categories of work?

429
00:36:13,440 --> 00:36:25,200
The tech product is the most central and we know that it's extremely important for the, you know, I oversee the technology and the product now and then I work very closelyely on the day to day for performance marketing.

430
00:36:25,680 --> 00:36:27,520
And we've hired a VP of marketing.

431
00:36:28,080 --> 00:36:30,400
He ran all of our lifecycle and retention marketing.

432
00:36:30,880 --> 00:36:32,880
And then now he's taking over that performance marketing.

433
00:36:33,200 --> 00:36:36,240
So I'm handing him over the reins and he's been doing a great job.

434
00:36:36,560 --> 00:36:40,400
So that was kind of the performance marketing was closely related to the technical side of the business.

435
00:36:40,960 --> 00:36:41,040
Yeah.

436
00:36:41,280 --> 00:36:43,840
So it was just all under one roof when we started.

437
00:36:44,080 --> 00:36:50,080
And so that made it easy because I had a lot of empathy with the marketers because I was the marketer.

438
00:36:50,480 --> 00:36:53,280
And so I was like, hey, tech team, and I'm running the tech team.

439
00:36:53,440 --> 00:36:55,040
I'm like, hey guys, I'm, I get it.

440
00:36:55,280 --> 00:36:55,680
I get it.

441
00:36:55,760 --> 00:37:00,160
It's, you know, we're getting another request and it's coming at you, you know, and you got to get it done yesterday.

442
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:10,640
So it created a lot of empatpathy where I was able to have a different perspective than probably most engineers that I got to feel the pain of needs from the marketing team where a lot of times when it's divided.

443
00:37:10,880 --> 00:37:22,880
And so really it was one cohesive whole and then we still operate even though I'm more of a partner transitioning it over, but it's still built into our DNA so we're closely aligned on that.

444
00:37:23,440 --> 00:37:28,160
And so really it's we don't really divide it up, but we know that there's a priority.

445
00:37:28,400 --> 00:37:57,680
Hey, if the tech product is not working correctly, if not sending the right signals, if it doesn't have the right flows, if something, you know, we're watching each step of the flow, if something' on the marketing side, if a creative goes sideways or gets dropped out of the list or something happens over there, if a change happens in Facebook platform or YouTube or whatever and one of the signals goes down, they have to adjust and then we, you know, we have to work in tandem.

446
00:37:57,920 --> 00:38:02,880
So it's a very unified front and we don't really divide it up like a normal organization.

447
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:19,040
I feel like that's one of the biggest things we've seen talking to larger customers who are considering migrating to RevenueCat, like existing apps that are large and want to use RevenueCat, is there's often a dysfunction between marketing.

448
00:38:19,440 --> 00:38:22,880
marketing and engineering where the marketing team is like fighting for resources.

449
00:38:23,680 --> 00:38:25,440
They're not tightly integrated.

450
00:38:26,080 --> 00:38:34,160
And similarly, like often very successful apps, performance rolled up to like a CTO or like a very technical or they built it into as part of the machine.

451
00:38:34,400 --> 00:38:37,920
This was a realization sort of mid cycle or mid into this journey for me.

452
00:38:38,160 --> 00:38:46,800
I talked to, I think it was somebody from Blinkist actually, and like how they did, because they were very successful early on this and, you know, it all ran up to their CTO, right?

453
00:38:47,040 --> 00:38:50,640
And he was, you know, he integrated like all this stuff and made sure it all ran.

454
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:57,600
And then you were saying, Ryan, just like elevating the urgency of those requests to engineers because like they don't always understand.

455
00:38:58,400 --> 00:39:06,240
And also I find too on marketing, sometimes marketers will totally hamstrung by something which to an engineer is trivial, right?

456
00:39:06,480 --> 00:39:12,160
It's like, uh, and it's just like, if you can connect that and like clear that barrier, like, suddenly you can unlock a lot of value.

457
00:39:12,320 --> 00:39:16,400
But I think businesses like yours, it's not app and then layer marketing on it, right?

458
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:19,600
Like the whole machine is like one big product, right?

459
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:23,520
It's one big business, which I don't know if everyone's figured that out yet.

460
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:30,080
And we talked to Jason Van de Merva on the podcast two years ago now, and he was head of growth engineering.

461
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:32,640
And it sounds like that's what you've built.

462
00:39:32,960 --> 00:39:36,960
And maybe that's like a big takeaway for a lot of organizations who have dysfunction.

463
00:39:37,760 --> 00:39:50,720
between marketing and engineering is like you need to like rethink how these teams work together because when you can pair growth and engineering together to be more effective, it's going to be way more effective.

464
00:39:51,120 --> 00:39:54,640
Do you have the engineers like dedicated to growth and performance marketing stuff?

465
00:39:54,960 --> 00:39:55,520
We actually don't.

466
00:39:56,080 --> 00:39:58,320
We don't have engineers dedicated towards it.

467
00:39:58,560 --> 00:40:00,880
It's more of everybody's a growth engineer.

468
00:40:01,120 --> 00:40:01,280
Right.

469
00:40:01,760 --> 00:40:05,040
I mean, at a venture back startup, yes, David, that's kind of true.

470
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:07,120
It's one road map.

471
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:08,800
So we don't have different road maps.

472
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:12,560
I think that's one of the key things that I learned is don't have different road maps.

473
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:16,480
Make sure that your product road map is taken into account.

474
00:40:17,360 --> 00:40:29,680
the whole organization and making sure that everyone's getting visibility into that and understanding the priority and priority shifts and then modeling out the ramifications of if we delay this, this is what we can expect.

475
00:40:30,240 --> 00:40:36,720
And so giving people more of the, I'm not the, you know, I wouldn't say I'm perfect on this, but that's the ideal state of we do have one roadmap.

476
00:40:36,880 --> 00:40:37,840
It's a living roadmap.

477
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:44,320
It's constantly changing and it's based on different stakeholders so that everyone's aware of what's coming.

478
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:55,360
And so that, you know, I think that's how we've solved it rather than saying, okay, there's going to be a team dedicated to this because when you're small and scrappy and I still consider us small and scrappy.

479
00:40:55,840 --> 00:41:04,080
We run a lead team is when there's some engineers over there that seem like they have a little bit of downtime and you you have a hey, come over here.

480
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:11,440
You end up in this like interlocking resource strains constraints where like one team will have a little bit extra resources and then but there's a team with the need.

481
00:41:12,160 --> 00:41:16,080
We're at this stage now where we have like multiple engineering teams and growing.

482
00:41:16,320 --> 00:41:30,800
And I think one of the things that's made it somewhat more successful is basically us having to go and do this being my CTO and I like reprioritizations like and just moving people around constantly to like because yeah, you lack that visibility and stuff.

483
00:41:30,960 --> 00:41:33,520
And but you know, I don't think anyone's got it figured out.

484
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:35,120
I love the one road map thing.

485
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:35,680
I don't know.

486
00:41:35,680 --> 00:41:37,200
I'm trying to get to zero road maps.

487
00:41:37,360 --> 00:41:38,400
I don't want any road maps.

488
00:41:38,560 --> 00:41:40,080
Just ask me what's our road map?

489
00:41:40,240 --> 00:41:41,600
It's whatever we're working on right now.

490
00:41:41,680 --> 00:41:42,640
Everything else is just ideas.

491
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:43,520
Hot take.

492
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:44,320
All right.

493
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:45,840
No road maps.

494
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:48,000
There's just today.

495
00:41:48,240 --> 00:41:49,440
We have to live in the moment, Ryan.

496
00:41:49,680 --> 00:42:04,960
You know, one tiny little example from what you were talking about that I just experienced like last week in my little indie app, my little weather app, I've known for like eighteen months that our onboarding sucks, but I wanted to focus on the product.

497
00:42:05,440 --> 00:42:13,280
And so I kind of ignored the onboarding and I like just put together like what I thought was like the simplest onboarding that my cousin who's doing development could possibly do.

498
00:42:14,080 --> 00:42:18,400
And so for like 18 months it's been like eating away at me like we gotta fix the onboarding.

499
00:42:18,480 --> 00:42:19,120
We gotta fix it.

500
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:20,400
But I never talked to him about it.

501
00:42:20,880 --> 00:42:28,080
And so just last week I finally like had the conversation like and laid out the whole thing of like okay, we have a hard paywall.

502
00:42:28,320 --> 00:42:29,200
Onboarding is everything.

503
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:32,560
And like we need to start running some cycles on onboarding.

504
00:42:32,720 --> 00:42:34,400
Here's like the expectations I see.

505
00:42:34,560 --> 00:42:38,160
Like if we improve onboarding, I think we can improve conversion by X-ray.

506
00:42:38,480 --> 00:42:42,400
And I had this whole like 40-minute conversation and lots of back and forth.

507
00:42:42,880 --> 00:42:52,080
And just having that conversation on that level to like get on the same team that, hey, we actually need to spend some cycles on this was a huge unlock.

508
00:42:52,640 --> 00:42:54,240
for us as a little two-person team.

509
00:42:54,640 --> 00:43:10,880
I can't imagine, like, as a bigger organization that is facing a lot of this dysfunction, even if you can't necessarily combine all the teams and create a growth engineer or whatever, just like break down those communication barriers between growth and engineering so that you're all on that same team and understand where those roadblocks are.

510
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:12,480
I try to break down the barriers, David.

511
00:43:12,800 --> 00:43:14,960
Just nobody wants to hear it when they actually know what I think.

512
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:15,760
That's the problem.

513
00:43:17,120 --> 00:43:17,760
They don't want to know.

514
00:43:18,080 --> 00:43:19,760
You don't want to know what the roadmap looks like.

515
00:43:20,080 --> 00:43:20,960
It's crazy in there.

516
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:22,480
Yeah.

517
00:43:22,880 --> 00:43:26,720
One question would be, how do you think about the product roadmap?

518
00:43:26,880 --> 00:43:29,840
And so we have a SaaS portion and a consumer portion.

519
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:34,800
You can kind of look at it as a two sided marketplace like an Etsy or something where we connect people and pastors.

520
00:43:35,440 --> 00:43:44,160
And I find that in consumer subscription world in a direct to consumer application, the roadmap can be a little more fuzzy than a SaaS.

521
00:43:44,640 --> 00:43:46,960
And so in the SaaS world, there's a lot more modeling.

522
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:56,720
There's a lot more math towards features because you understand, like, A, if we roll out this product, we can have a net revenue retention increase because we can now upsell for this.

523
00:43:56,800 --> 00:43:58,720
And we think there's going to be a three percent adoption rate.

524
00:43:59,040 --> 00:44:06,560
You can do that a little bit in consumer, but when I've talked to other D to C companies, I find that that's less prevalent than in the SaaS world.

525
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:13,840
So how do you, think about it at RevenueCat about your roadmap and modeling?

526
00:44:14,160 --> 00:44:14,240
Yeah.

527
00:44:14,400 --> 00:44:18,480
I mean, I think that really depends on how you're pricing, which is the reason it doesn't work in consumer as well.

528
00:44:18,640 --> 00:44:25,360
It's because like some SaaS is can do really nice à la carte pricing where you're like, okay, you want feature X is our core feature.

529
00:44:25,440 --> 00:44:26,400
And then you want feature Y.

530
00:44:27,360 --> 00:44:31,760
Okay, we'll just add on, you know, another charge per seed or whatever to your contract.

531
00:44:31,920 --> 00:44:35,280
And so you can very easily go, okay, we think twenty percent of our customers will use this.

532
00:44:35,360 --> 00:44:36,800
It's ten thousand dollars a year.

533
00:44:36,960 --> 00:44:38,240
Bang, like that's our value.

534
00:44:38,560 --> 00:44:39,600
And there it's pretty nice.

535
00:44:39,840 --> 00:44:47,440
Also, usually that's coupled with a like pretty understood B2B Salesforce where you're like, I know how to get these folks to convert to this.

536
00:44:47,600 --> 00:44:48,960
And you can incentivize people to do that.

537
00:44:49,280 --> 00:44:49,360
too.

538
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:52,480
It's like, hey, sell this new ten thousand dollar widget add on.

539
00:44:52,640 --> 00:44:54,000
And I think you'd be surprised.

540
00:44:54,080 --> 00:45:05,200
Like, I would love to know, like, a Salesforce maybe is like the extreme example, but like, I'd say, mid to late stage mid cap SaaS companies, I would bet the majority of their Salesforce is not like selling Salesforce.

541
00:45:05,360 --> 00:45:07,520
It's selling cross whatever, right?

542
00:45:07,760 --> 00:45:08,800
It's all about just expanding.

543
00:45:08,880 --> 00:45:11,120
It's, it has nothing to do now revenue cap.

544
00:45:11,360 --> 00:45:16,000
We actually, our specific case is different because just because the way we price is like mostly based on volume.

545
00:45:16,400 --> 00:45:20,240
So it's like a little bit harder because it's like, well, we price on volume for everything else.

546
00:45:20,400 --> 00:45:26,080
So it's like you add in a new thing and it just for us, it ends up getting kind of baked out in like how much pressure we get.

547
00:45:26,240 --> 00:45:32,000
We can, you know, in like a contract negotiation or whatever if we're adding features and things like that, but it's a little bit less a la carte.

548
00:45:32,480 --> 00:45:35,200
I need a mixed media modeling model for it, right?

549
00:45:35,440 --> 00:45:39,840
I just know if I build a more useful product, like people will pay for it more, pay more for it.

550
00:45:40,000 --> 00:45:43,280
And then I just figure that like a little bit of horse trading will find the right price.

551
00:45:43,680 --> 00:45:44,960
So I don't benefit from that modeling.

552
00:45:45,040 --> 00:45:48,080
So I don't have a roadmap why I'm just like, man, we'll just build whatever.

553
00:45:48,240 --> 00:45:52,400
But to that point too, like sometimes you'll have like key customers that will be like, we need X.

554
00:45:52,880 --> 00:45:56,320
We will literally sign a contract for Y if you guys build X today.

555
00:45:56,480 --> 00:45:58,000
And like, okay, now we have a roadmap.

556
00:45:58,080 --> 00:46:01,200
Like this is getting built right now because it's kind of like, it's a burden to hand.

557
00:46:01,760 --> 00:46:07,680
But yeah, I just feel like generally, and I think, I don't know if this is true out if this is like a B2B thing.

558
00:46:07,840 --> 00:46:09,200
I think it's kind of true for all businesses.

559
00:46:09,360 --> 00:46:25,120
It's kind of actually, I think the philosophical underpinnings of media mix modeling is just there's so much, so many inputs to these systems and like so many uncertainties that I think if you try to overly simplify and be like, oh, this lever moves this number.

560
00:46:25,440 --> 00:46:29,600
And if you like, over focus on that stuff, I think you can end up driving to like really odd conclusions.

561
00:46:30,320 --> 00:46:33,360
There's probably some relationship, right, between lever A and output B.

562
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:39,840
But if you're not considering everything else, I find this too with like, I think you see this a lot in like funnel, funnel optimization, right?

563
00:46:40,160 --> 00:46:42,400
It's like, okay, you move your paywall from the beginning to the end.

564
00:46:42,640 --> 00:46:48,960
Like you were saying, yeah, you'll change or from the end to the beginning, you'll change your con conversion rate, but you're going to it's going to have other effects, right?

565
00:46:49,440 --> 00:46:54,320
And which may or may not be localized to that change, which then makes it hard to manage and measure.

566
00:46:54,480 --> 00:46:57,680
So you have to make sure you're measuring multiple data points.

567
00:46:57,840 --> 00:47:00,400
And then I still think there's like, I don't know, this might be just personal.

568
00:47:00,720 --> 00:47:02,400
I feel like there's still room for craft, right?

569
00:47:02,560 --> 00:47:07,600
There's still room for like gut feel and being like, meh, like I think this feels better and like we should do this.

570
00:47:07,840 --> 00:47:10,240
But as much as I'm, I look at dashboards all day.

571
00:47:10,400 --> 00:47:11,280
I'm very data driven.

572
00:47:11,520 --> 00:47:12,560
But, you know, I don't know.

573
00:47:12,720 --> 00:47:17,600
If we don't have, if we don't have some human magic going into product creation, the AIs are just going to replace us.

574
00:47:17,680 --> 00:47:18,800
And wouldn't that be terrible?

575
00:47:19,120 --> 00:47:21,600
Well, you know, it depends what magic is, you know.

576
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:26,480
We might find out that our magic wasn't, as magical as we thought.

577
00:47:26,720 --> 00:47:27,760
Is that magic after all?

578
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:28,480
No, no.

579
00:47:28,720 --> 00:47:31,200
See, as soon as you have a road map, you're replaceable.

580
00:47:31,360 --> 00:47:33,840
So, like, no road map can be replaced by AI.

581
00:47:34,080 --> 00:47:36,480
How do you manage the B to C road map, Ryan?

582
00:47:36,640 --> 00:47:40,880
I think a lot of people will be curious about that at your stage and given all that we've discussed.

583
00:47:41,200 --> 00:47:49,840
You mean like features specifically, David, or like just B to C, like because we kind of like did this chase a rabbit on the B to B side, but most of our listeners are B to C.

584
00:47:50,080 --> 00:47:56,000
So I wanted to kind of bring it back to like, what do you do on the B to C side that maybe folks listening can learn from?

585
00:47:56,240 --> 00:48:02,480
When it comes to the B to C side, I really think of subscription is kind of your core for your monetization.

586
00:48:03,280 --> 00:48:08,000
But my general thoughts are post IDFA subscription is not enough.

587
00:48:08,480 --> 00:48:12,640
I think Duolingo is a perfect case study of subscription plus ads.

